t":"Affects Medicare beneficiaries receiving outpatient prescription drugs from physician practices, participating physicians, group practices, and pharmacy networks.","evidence":"furnished to an individual during the period beginning on January 1, 2026, and ending on December 31, 2030... dispensed by the referring physician... from a building described","evidence_verified":false,"key":"population","score":50},{"assessment":"Alters federal physician self-referral restrictions for specific medical practices and outpatient drug dispensing, impacting how certain Medicare patients access medications.","evidence":"To amend title XVIII of the Social Security Act to establish an exception to the physician self-referral prohibition for certain outpatient prescription drugs furnished by a physician practice","evidence_verified":true,"key":"magnitude","score":40},{"assessment":"Includes a $18,000,000 offset by reducing the Medicare Improvement Fund from $1,804,000,000 to $1,786,000,000.","evidence":"Section 1898(b)(1) of the Social Security Act (42 U.S.C. 1395iii(b)(1)) is amended by striking '1,804,000,000' and inserting '1,786,000,000'.","evidence_verified":false,"key":"fiscal","score":30},{"assessment":"Creates a temporary statutory exception to the Stark physician self-referral law and mandates a comprehensive GAO study and report on pharmacy integration and arrangements.","evidence":"The Comptroller General of the United States... shall conduct a study examining-- (A) pharmacies or pharmacy networks... that, after the date of the enactment of this section, dispense significantly more... covered part D drugs","evidence_verified":false,"key":"scope","score":40},{"assessment":"Provides a temporary carve-out effective from January 1, 2026, through December 31, 2030, alongside a one-time GAO study and report due within 3 years.","evidence":"furnished to an individual during the period beginning on January 1, 2026, and ending on December 31, 2030... Not later than 3 years afte